RRFS CI Design and Usage Guide                                                
Target Audience: Code Managers and Repository Administrators                  

This document outlines the technical design, architectural framework, and     
usage of the automated GitHub Actions CI pipelines established to enforce     
NCEP Central Operations (NCO) Implementation Standards.
These CI pipelines enforce only a subset of the NCO Implementation Standards,
 as not all standards can be fully automated through a CI process.                       

System Architecture Design                                                    
![CI Architecture Diagram](ci_design_diagram.png)                             

Core Design Pattern                                                           
Every new CI test adheres to a strict standard to maintain uniformity across  
the repository:                                                               

Workflow File (`.yml`): Named                                                 
`NCO_Consistency_${CI_Test_Job_Name}.yml`. Controls the GitHub Action runner. 

Exclusion List (`.lst`): Named                                                
`exclude_file_list_${CI_Test_Job_Name}.lst`. Used by Code Managers to         
permanently bypass specific files/directories from tests.                     

Regression Test (`.sh`): Named                                                
`regression_test_${CI_Test_Job_Name}.sh`. A localized sandbox to safely test  
custom linter logic without risking live repository data.                     

Zero-Match Logic: If a branch lacks matching target files, the CI completes   
successfully (exit 0) and logs "No files found to check. Exiting gracefully". 

Artifact Generation: Output is logged to `ci_run.log`, uploaded as an         
artifact, and rendered in the `$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY`.                         

---                                                                           

Detailed CI Testing Specifications                                            

1. Bash Consistency (`NCO_Consistency_Bash`)                                  

Technical Design Overview: Multi-stage bash verification using `shellcheck`   
and custom regular expressions. Enforces standard conventions: a              
`#!/bin/bash` shebang, `set -x` for operational traceability, and strictly    
prohibits unconditional `exit 0` at the EOF to prevent suppressed error codes.

Usage: Targets `.sh`/`.bash` files and the `./jobs`, `./scripts` directories. 
Governed by `exclude_file_list_Bash.lst`.

2. Implementation Standard (`NCO_Consistency_Implementation_Standard`)        

Technical Design Overview: Enforces NCO EE2 directory layouts utilizing a     
custom Python linter (`linter_nco_implementation_standard.py`). Prevents the  
creation of subdirectories directly beneath `./jobs` and `./scripts`. Scans   
scripts to prevent hardcoded paths (`/lfs`, `/gpfs`), banned commands         
(`> /dev/null`), and validates `err_chk` implementation.                      

Usage: Prevents deployment blockers before code merges into operational       
branches. Handled via `exclude_file_list_Implementation_Standard.lst`.        

3. Python Consistency (`NCO_Consistency_Python`)                              

Technical Design Overview: Implements Python code quality governance          
utilizing `flake8` for PEP-8 compliance and `pytest` (with `pytest-cov`) for  
unit testing. Features dynamic path scanning to automatically ignore virtual  
environments (`venv`, `build`, `dist`).                                       

Usage: Validates workflow generation tools. Managed via                       
`exclude_file_list_python.lst`.                                               

Regression Test: Executed via                                                 
`./regression_test_python_Implementation_Standard.sh` to ensure the         
duplicate-key checking logic functions properly in the runner environment.

4. Yamllint Standard (`NCO_Consistency_Yamllint`)                             

Technical Design Overview: Uses the standard Python `yamllint` utility to     
ensure foundational syntax correctness across configuration files. Enforces   
a maximum line length of 120 characters.                                      

Usage: GitHub workflows (`.github/*`) are excluded by default to avoid syntax 
rules clashing with GitHub Actions syntax.

5. Yamllint Scientific Hierarchical                                           
(`NCO_Consistency_Yamllint_Scientific_Hierarchical`)                          

Technical Design Overview: Deploys a custom PyYAML `UniqueKeyLoader`.         
Designed to catch duplicate keys within configuration blocks. Standard        
parsers often overwrite duplicate keys silently, which can cause catastrophic 
parameter failures in JEDI DA/MPAS models.                                    

Usage: Crucial for scientific configuration integrity. Unlike the standard    
Yamllint, this explicitly checks GitHub workflows to ensure CI parameter      
integrity.                                                                    

